# Upstream embeds a pre-generated, minified copy of its web UI from
# github.com/kopia/htmluibuild. The original UI source is a separate
# repository; Source3 is the kopia/htmlui tree matching the assets shipped
# in htmluibuild (the htmluibuild commit message records the htmlui commit
# e9f6ace72ea3a9edd4f8526e133885afa33c42e7). Fedora requires the source for
# pre-generated code to be present in the source package. Regenerating the
# bundle would require the complete npm/vite dependency chain, which is not
# packaged in Fedora, so the pre-generated bundle is retained as a
# documented significant-hardship case under the JavaScript guidelines.
# The bundled npm libraries are declared with Provides below and their
# license texts are installed from Source4.
# https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/packaging-guidelines/what-can-be-packaged/#pregenerated-code
# https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/packaging-guidelines/JavaScript/

# Two categories of upstream tests need skipping so %%check is deterministic:
# 1) Broken by Go 1.27 (Fedora 45 / rawhide) stdlib changes:
#      - TestMetrics_CompressibleData (repo): compress/flate output 36 -> 33 bytes
#      - TestFormatV1/V2/TestCompression_CompressibleData (repo/content): 79 -> 75
#      - TestBuildFromExtraError (repo/maintenancestats): encoding/json v2 makes
#        "[]" -> "null", changing the asserted error text
# 2) Upstream data race (architecture flaky, seen on fedora-45-ppc64le):
#      - TestFormatV1/V2/TestFlushWaitsForAllPendingWriters: a goroutine calls
#        t.Errorf after the parent test has completed, so Go reports
#        "Fail in goroutine after test has completed"
# Go 1.27 fixes are upstream commit 56ff291 (#5681); the race has no fix yet.
# 3) Statistically flaky upstream test:
#      - TestPickActionWeighted (tests/robustness/engine) draws 100000
#        weighted samples and asserts that each count lands within 10 percent
#        of the expected value. For the A:B:C = 1:10:100 case the expected
#        count for A is 900.9 with a standard deviation of ~29.9, so the 10
#        percent band is only about 3 standard deviations wide and the test
#        fails roughly 0.27 percent of the time. The F43 x86_64 build hit it
#        (expected 900.9, actual 810), while the identical source passed on
#        F44/F45/F46. Upstream widened the tolerance to 15 percent after
#        0.23.1. The package carries a "darwin || (linux && amd64)" build tag,
#        so it only ever runs on x86_64.
# Only those tests are skipped; everything else still runs. Note: gocheck2
# combines the -s values into a single go test -skip expression, where "/"
# separates parent and subtest names, so the full subtest paths are passed
# below.

# The end-to-end suite is heavy on slow architectures and can exceed Go's
# default 10 minute per-package test timeout. The tests/end_to_end_test
# package takes about 134s on x86_64 and 401s on ppc64le, but on s390x it
# exceeded 600s and the build failed with "panic: test timed out after 10m0s".
# The timeout is raised below rather than skipping those tests.

# ppc64le uses 64K filesystem blocks, equal to iocopy.BufSize. The sparse
# restore test assumes a smaller block size, underflows an offset, and then
# checks the wrong physical allocation sizes.
%ifarch ppc64le
%global skip_ppc64le_tests -s TestSnapshotSparseRestore
%endif

# This randomized stress test has been observed to fail on s390x with a
# deleted-flag mismatch (upstream has no deterministic fix yet).
%ifarch s390x
%global skip_s390x_tests -s TestIndexBlobManagerStress
%endif

